# truncate — real-world examples

## Empty a file in place

```sh
# Clear the contents of a file (file stays, size becomes 0)
truncate -s 0 file.txt

# Homelab: clear a runaway log without disturbing the process writing to it
sudo truncate -s 0 "/var/lib/plexmediaserver/Library/Application Support/Plex Media Server/Logs/Plex Media Server.log"
```

## Shrink or grow to an exact size

```sh
# Truncate a file to exactly 100 bytes
truncate -s 100 file.txt

# Truncate a file to 100 KB
# (M, G, T, P, E, Z, and Y may be used in place of "K" as required)
truncate -s 100K file.txt

# Create a 10 GB sparse file instantly (e.g. a test disk image for virt-manager)
truncate -s 10G /var/lib/libvirt/images/test-disk.raw
```

## Relative sizes

```sh
# Shrink by 1 MB / grow by 512 bytes
truncate -s -1M bigfile.bin
truncate -s +512 bigfile.bin
```

## Other ways to empty a file

```sh
> access.log             # redirect nothing into the file
: > access.log           # same, using the : builtin
true > access.log

cat /dev/null > access.log
cp /dev/null access.log
dd if=/dev/null of=access.log
echo -n "" > access.log
```

## Flag reference

| Flag | Meaning |
|------|---------|
| `-s SIZE` | set size (prefix `+`/`-` for relative; K/M/G... suffixes) |
| `-r FILE` | use FILE's size as the reference size |
| `-c` | do not create files that don't exist |
| `-o` | treat SIZE as number of I/O blocks |
